# Kinetiq Modules 07B — Smart catalogue synchronization

Apply these complete files over the repository root, including the hidden `.github` directory. Keep existing `modules/`, `catalog/upstreams.json`, `catalog/local-modules.json`, `repository.json`, `package.json`, and `scripts/check-unified.mjs`. Do not install this archive into Kinetiq itself. It is a source update for Haimiya612/Kinetiq-Modules only.

The existing workflow file is replaced, not duplicated. Its schedule is twice hourly (GitHub can delay scheduled jobs). It publishes metadata on main after tests and validation, without waiting for a PR. GitHub must allow Actions to write repository contents and push to main; protected branches may prevent this. A failed push does not rewrite main; the generated catalogue artifact remains available. No executable upstream module is run or republished by this job. Existing package URLs and hashes remain upstream references. Package hash authenticity, code safety, runtime support and content rights are not independently established by catalogue validation.

Selection: exact module ID plus matching family/identity/identity number, valid HTTPS URL/hash/SemVer, stable production preference, numeric prerelease ordering, same-owner testing candidates for beta-track modules, explicit optional testing override per module, local ownership preference, no silent downgrade or same-version checksum replacement, and explicit retirement/withdrawal or owner removal. Duplicate candidates and decisions stay in diagnostics. Unavailable candidates retain their previous published references, labelled retained/unverified in unified.json. A retained reference may be unavailable or stale; it is not a rollback package or proof of availability. To intentionally remove a module, add its ID to `removeModuleIds` in `catalog/update-policy.json`. To opt a module into newer testing candidates, add its ID to `allowTestingUpdatesFor`. An older previously selected candidate is not automatically downgraded merely by changing the channel policy. Do not auto-install unreviewed executable packages on devices.

On a successful first sync, remote index snapshots are saved in `catalog/snapshots/`. Subsequent fetch failures can use validated last-known snapshots; the first sync needs all upstreams available. Snapshots contain public catalogue metadata, not package binaries. Non-video formats remain discoverable in unified.json; current native video installer must not be fed incompatible formats. A separate Kinetiq-iOS update is required for native manga/connector/AnyGo adapters, automatic app update checks, per-device compatibility/health, rollback of actual packages, and investigation of the 39-vs-43 displayed count.

## iPhone workflow
1. Extract archive and upload the replacement files to the exact paths (including `.github/workflows/unified-catalog.yml`).
2. Commit to main. In GitHub Settings → Actions → General verify workflow write permissions if allowed. Do not bypass required branch reviews; if direct push is blocked, use the artifact and manually review/commit or use a PR.
3. Actions → Kinetiq Unified Module Catalogue → Run workflow if the push did not trigger it. Confirm test, sync, check, artifact, and publish steps.
4. Check `repository.json`, `catalog/diagnostics.json`, and `catalog/unified.json` on main. Reopen/refresh the existing repository in Kinetiq. Compare the app's available count with the selected candidate count, not total entries.

